# Notify

Send $ARGUMENTS as plate-bot. A message only the team can read proceeds without asking, as `AGENTS.md`'s Team rules say, so draft it, send it and report it.

1. Pick the recipient. It is a GitHub login on `team` in `.agents/pstack.json` when that person must act or answer, `dev` when every teammate must act, or `channel` for news nobody acts on, such as a demo or an updated clip. A `channel` post pings nobody (owner's word, 2026-11-20). A session that lands a change with a `Team:` line leaves its demo for zbot instead, as `AGENTS.md`'s Team rule says, so the team sees it once. A triage run's retest message attaches its fix's demo itself, because that fix lands with no Team line. A pasted Discord message goes back to its author. When the person is not on `team`, stop and say so, because the send refuses them.
2. Draft the text in the voice of `.agents/pstack/rules/writing.md`, as one post for a teammate with no summary block and no link list. The reader is a teammate who works through Claude. Link each demo or docs page the text names on beta.platejs.org, such as https://beta.platejs.org/blocks/field-demo, so the reader opens it in one click; the send refuses a demo or docs page named without its link.
   - Lead with the result, then the one thing the reader does, when the post asks them to act.
   - Name an issue or PR as a Markdown link with its number and title, such as `[#132 <title>](<url>)`. Read the title with `gh issue view <N> --repo <owner/repo> --json title -q .title`. When a comment asks the reader to act, such as a retest request, link that comment's URL.
   - Leave out what the reader already knows, such as "on beta" in a retest notice, because every retest runs on beta.
   - When the reader must act, name the skill command that does it, such as `/triage retest plate-private#132`. Say what to ask Claude for only when no skill does it. A shell command never goes in the text, and the send refuses one in backticks.
3. Write the text to a file in the run directory or the scratchpad. Run `node .agents/pstack/writing-check.mjs <file>` and fix each line it prints.
4. Show it. When a message is about something a reader sees in the app, show it, and make every clip and before-and-after image with the `clip` skill (never send before and after as two separate files). A change to how something behaves gets one before-and-after clip; a change to how it looks at rest gets one before-and-after image; something new in motion gets one short clip; something new at rest gets a still cropped to the feature. Read each still, a clip's own stills included, before you send it, and send only what shows what the message claims. Attach at most four files. Take stills from your own proof, or take fresh screenshots of the page.
5. Send it with `node tooling/scripts/notify.mjs send --to <login, dev or channel> [--file <path>]... [--no-media "<reason>"] "$(cat <file>)"`, one `--file` per PNG, JPG, GIF, MP4 or WebM, each at most 20 MiB and 25 MiB together. The send refuses a beta.platejs.org link or UI commit with no `--file` unless `--no-media "<reason>"` gives a non-visual reason. Write it as `$(cat <file>)` even when the text looks plain, because an issue title can hold a backtick or `$`. Never use `pnpm notify send`, because pnpm hands its arguments to `sh` inside double quotes, so each backticked span or `$` in the text runs as a command. The send links each commit hash in the text to that commit on GitHub. It refuses a hash that neither this checkout nor its companion has pushed, so write the link yourself for a commit of another repository. A post holds at most Discord's 2,000 characters, so split a longer text into several files and send each one.
6. Tell the owner who got it and the exact text. When the command prints `skipped:`, say so instead, because the same text with the same files went to that recipient in the last 24 hours.
